WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.763
- It smells kind of gross but,

00:00:01.843 --> 00:00:04.223
we're saving the planet
so that's the priority.

00:00:04.258 --> 00:00:06.675
(soft music)

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:11.560
- 40% of food goes uneaten
by Americans every year.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.560
Americans are throwing
away 165 billion dollars

00:00:16.741 --> 00:00:19.270
in food every year.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.070
Meanwhile 42 million Americans,

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.150
13 million of which are
children, don't have access

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.020
to enough food to maintain
a healthy lifestyle.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.350
I'm Merle, I'm a Tasty
producer here in New York.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:31.791
And hearing all of those facts,

00:00:31.791 --> 00:00:33.160
is terrifying.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.680
So what are we gonna do about it?

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.220
Here in New York we have
eight active Tasty producers

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.290
that are regularly filming
and testing recipes.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.890
So we go through a lot of food

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.680
and that means a lot of food
scraps end up in the trash.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.700
So for the past week I
collected all of the food waste

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.493
we produced and I weighed it.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.733
61 pounds?

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.660
That number is so scary because
if you multiply that by four

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:57.445
that's 244 pounds a month

00:00:57.445 --> 00:00:59.120
and if you multiply that by 12,

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.230
that's 2,928 pounds a year.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.660
That's a lot of food waste.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.960
I can't help but think there's
more we could do about it.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.830
I am here today because I'm challenging

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.470
our Tasty producers to produce

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.460
zero food waste for seven days.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.523
Both in work and at home.

00:01:15.556 --> 00:01:16.389
- Oh man,

00:01:16.389 --> 00:01:18.110
- That sounds difficult.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.730
Like what does it entail though?

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.790
- While you're at work
and while you're at home

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.630
instead of throwing your scraps away

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.200
you're gonna be composting everything.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.820
And also like just be more
mindful about how much food

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.230
you're ordering, how much
food you're using for shoots.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.944
So will you pledge to take this challenge

00:01:31.944 --> 00:01:33.363
with your signature?

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.420
- Alright, fine, yes.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.300
- Easy, lets do it.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.350
No like blood pledge?

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:37.543
- That's later.

00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:38.480
I can't do that on camera.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:39.750
- It's gonna be challenging

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.566
but I think it'll be really rewarding.

00:01:41.566 --> 00:01:42.660
- It's a good challenge.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.990
- This positivity is honestly
gonna make me so emotional.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.370
After spending the morning
hunting everyone down,

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:50.110
I finally got all of them
to sign away their rights

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.613
to waste any food for the next week.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.830
It would be impossible to
cook the amount of food

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.980
that we do and come out of
it with zero food byproducts.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.940
So I encourage producers
first and foremost

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.200
to limit their intake of
food to only what they need.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.940
And to give away any extra
food to those in need

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.480
or those even around the office.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.970
Composting was only supposed
to be used as a last resort.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.570
- So I've tasked myself
with eating this apple

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.010
with as little waste as possible.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.050
I'm gonna compost it and
I feel so much better

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.750
about myself it's crazy.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.795
- So I have this extra batch
of sweet potato hummus,

00:02:25.795 --> 00:02:28.247
instead of throwing it
out I'm going to save it

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:31.560
and maybe eat it as a snack tomorrow.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.820
- I decided the most
fool proof way to ensure

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.235
we would consistently
compost in the Tasty kitchen

00:02:36.235 --> 00:02:38.520
was to organize a compost program.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.800
So I decided to take it upon myself

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.303
to try and make that happen.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.297
I called at least nine
different companies,

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:47.740
researched all afternoon
and talked about compost

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.056
for a couple of hours
until I finally found

00:02:50.056 --> 00:02:52.513
one woman who seemed like she could help.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:53.573
- [Meredith] This is Meredith.

00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:57.290
- Hi Meredith, my name
is Merle, I'm interested

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.533
in bringing composting to
my company that I work at.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.984
- [Meredith] Awesome,
that's great to hear Merle.

00:03:02.984 --> 00:03:06.105
- We talked for 30 minutes
about how to make this happen,

00:03:06.105 --> 00:03:08.409
toward the end I was
feeling pretty hopeful.

00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:09.467
(squeals)

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.590
I think it's gonna happen,
oh my god I'm so excited.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.220
Okay, so I'm waiting to talk
to facilities manager today

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.270
about bringing composting into the office.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.790
Very nervous, but after
my phone call yesterday

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.110
with Meredith I'm feeling pretty good.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.731
But I don't know what she's gonna say so,

00:03:22.731 --> 00:03:24.573
wish me luck.

00:03:24.978 --> 00:03:25.870
(exhales)

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.790
I just talked to facilities,
we're gonna be able to get

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:33.230
a compost situation rolling
out for the Tasty kitchen

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.870
which is great news, I don't know how this

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:37.290
is happening right now but
we're making it work people,

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.730
we're gonna be composting,
it's gonna be great.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.143
We're gonna officially have
composting in the Tasty kitchen.

00:03:42.143 --> 00:03:42.976
- What?

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.223
- [Merle] Forever.

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:45.056
- Are you serious?

00:03:45.056 --> 00:03:46.370
This makes it so much easier,

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:48.458
this is all I've ever
wanted this entire time.

00:03:48.458 --> 00:03:50.730
'cause if you give people the ability

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.280
and make it easy for them, they'll do it,

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:53.980
it's just when it's an inconvenience.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:55.963
- [Merle] I was so happy to
see that people were excited

00:03:55.963 --> 00:03:59.121
at the idea of having a
compost system in the kitchen.

00:03:59.121 --> 00:04:02.080
So the next step was to
have Meredith come in

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.208
and talk compost with us.

00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:05.047
- Why is composting important?

00:04:05.047 --> 00:04:08.970
Every one eats, so everyone
generates food waste,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.363
food scraps go to a
landfill, they get suffocated

00:04:11.363 --> 00:04:14.090
and in that environment which we refer to

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.450
as an anaerobic environment,
one without oxygen,

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.220
methane is a byproduct,
methane is a greenhouse gas.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.450
It's more potent than
carbon dioxide in terms

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.690
of it's warming potential and
it's a pretty easy solution.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.200
Removing food scraps from our waste stream

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.923
to avoid methane production
and to mitigate further

00:04:30.923 --> 00:04:33.230
you know green house gas
emissions and climate change

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.980
is very important and if
we all can play a part

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.210
in that process by putting our food scraps

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.670
in a separate bin in our houses,

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:40.600
why wouldn't we?

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.160
- [Merle] Meredith talked to our producers

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.530
to make sure they knew
how to properly compost

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.933
before she handed over our shiny new bin.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.273
- [Meredith] Thanks for you time guys.

00:04:48.273 --> 00:04:49.106
(audience applause)

00:04:49.106 --> 00:04:51.130
- I use Merle's compost
bin, it really wasn't hard

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.600
or scary and I don't
know why I was so scared.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.940
There's no reason not to be doing it.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.100
- I just got to say I feel like the world

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.900
is a little bit of better place now.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.280
I'm not taking all the credit,

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.010
but I'm taking a little
piece of the credit.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.970
- Meredith said that
actually one of the best ways

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.600
to reduce food waste is to eat it.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.280
So that's what we're gonna go do

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.793
is eat some Tasty leftovers.

00:05:09.821 --> 00:05:10.654
- That's what I been doing.

00:05:10.654 --> 00:05:11.487
- Yeah.
- Yes.

00:05:11.487 --> 00:05:13.320
- I forgot to compost.

00:05:14.663 --> 00:05:17.080
Ew, this is my walk of shame.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.650
- So heres a lot of the stuff
that we've gone through,

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:25.600
freezing it also helps
to keep the odors at bay

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.100
when I'm opening and adding to it.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.443
Also look at how crazy this is,

00:05:28.443 --> 00:05:31.683
this is a day and a half worth of compost.

00:05:31.683 --> 00:05:35.010
Here we have glorious Union
Square Farmer's Market,

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:36.810
look what I found, all this compost.

00:05:37.224 --> 00:05:39.024
- I just cleaned out my refrigerator

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.849
and I filled up one Tupperware container.

00:05:41.849 --> 00:05:43.781
I'm gonna put it into a
paper bag and walk it up

00:05:43.781 --> 00:05:45.693
to the farmers market.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.040
- I have some food waste
that I grated the other day.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.170
I am attempting to put
them in the composting bin

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:52.623
but I think that maybe I can
make something out of them.

00:05:52.623 --> 00:05:54.280
I actually made quite
a pretty tasty salad.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.380
Now I'm gonna see who I can share it with.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.140
- [Rachel] Since they have composting all

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.130
of these perfectly good
leftovers, we're gonna give them

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:02.870
away to people at the office.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:05.210
- Okay, I love my team, love them to death

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.750
but I'm not a fool, I'm
gonna check the trash bins

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:10.293
to make sure that nothing goes undetected.

00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:11.734
What the hell?

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:13.623
Wait, it's a full orange.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.760
I knew there would be some
slip ups along the way

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.280
which is totally fine, and to be expected

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.853
but they were not going
to elude the trash lady.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.433
Not on my watch.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.880
Just like anything else,
composting needs to become

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.960
a habit and soon enough
it'll be second nature.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.880
So it's time to weigh
any trash that people

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.240
accidentally accumulated

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.863
that would be going to landfill.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.430
As a whole, the Tasty kitchen
looks sparkling clean,

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.924
everyone's done a fantastic
job of trying to keep

00:06:41.924 --> 00:06:45.480
and use our produce to
it's fullest potential.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.130
And if not just to give it away.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.980
We're gonna compare this
number with our number

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:51.653
from last week which was 61 pounds.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:57.113
The verdict is in and we
got three and a half pounds.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.330
From 61 pounds to three and
a half pounds is amazing.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.740
I'm so happy for everyone,
I'm really proud of us.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.840
- All of the food that goes
to waste in the Tasty kitchen

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:08.960
I'm just shocked that we
weren't doing this before.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.380
- It's really nice to know that anything

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.699
that doesn't get eaten or
that people leave behind,

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:17.649
that ordinarily I would just throw away,

00:07:17.649 --> 00:07:20.550
can be repurposed in some fashion.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.230
- [Merle] Kermit the frog once said,

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:23.730
it's not easy being green.

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:26.353
But I'm here to tell you, oh yes it is.

